  "primary_subject_agency_ids": [],
  "summary": "The ACLU documents a pattern of Flock Safety misleading city councils, police departments, and the public \u2014 including lying to Oshkosh's city council about heat-map tracking (leading the council to revoke its contract one day after approval), misrepresenting federal data-sharing with CBP/DHS, denying ICE access to data, overselling its abortion-search safeguards, and falsely claiming ACLU partnerships. The piece urges governments to avoid contracting with Flock.",
  "key_quotes": [
    {
      "quote": "Ultimately, the Oshkosh City Council voted to immediately revoke its approval, thereby setting a record for the shortest time between a city approving and cancelling a Flock contract: one day.",
      "paragraph_idx": 9
    },
    {
      "quote": "After contradictory information later came to light, the company was forced to admit that it did, in fact, have contracts with U.S. Customs and Border Protection (CBP) and Homeland Security (DHS) for pilot projects that gave those agencies direct access to license data.",
      "paragraph_idx": 14
    },
    {
      "quote": "To be clear, neither the ACLU nor any of our affiliates have ever partnered with Flock Safety or worked with them to design any ALPR system.",
      "paragraph_idx": 21
    }
  ],
